      "Holding Client Money: Custody",
      "Custody means holding client money or securities, or having the power to get at them, like writing checks, deducting fees, or acting as trustee. Discretion alone is not custody if the adviser cannot take or withdraw the assets. Under NASAA rules, taking more than $500 in fees six or more months ahead can count as custody. Under the federal Investment Advisers Act, prepaid fees alone are not custody.\n\nAn adviser with custody must:\n• Keep client assets with a qualified custodian, like a bank or broker-dealer, separate from its own. Mixing them is commingling, which is banned.\n• Make sure clients get account statements at least every three months, usually from the custodian.\n• Have a surprise outside audit at least once a year, reported to the Administrator.\n• Follow client instructions promptly.\n• Tell clients in writing where and how their assets are held, and about any change.\nIt must also disclose custody on Form ADV and meet higher net capital requirements.\n\nTo avoid custody, an adviser must not hold client funds, securities, or checks payable to others for more than three business days, and must keep a record of anything it held briefly.\n\nBroker-dealers with custody must keep fully paid client securities apart from their own and from margin collateral. Agents may never take custody of client assets.",
