      "s65-c9-discretion",
      "Discretion and Trading Authority",
      "Time and price discretion is when the client picks the security, buy or sell, and amount, and lets the professional pick the moment and price. It can be given verbally and must be used by the end of that trading day.\n\nFull discretion means the professional decides what to buy or sell, how much, and when. That makes it a discretionary account, which needs written authority.\n• An adviser or IAR can start trading on verbal permission, but must get written authority within 10 business days after the first discretionary trade.\n• A broker-dealer or agent needs written authority before the first discretionary trade.\nEven with discretion, the client can still make their own decisions, and the professional must follow legitimate instructions.\n\nA client can also let someone else, not their agent or IAR, trade the account. This third-party trading authorization must be in writing, through a power of attorney. A limited authorization allows trading only. A full authorization also allows withdrawals.\n\nA spouse cannot trade or get account information without the account owner's written authority. Requests from regulators, government agencies, or courts must be honored.",
