      "s65-c9-prudent",
      "The Prudent Investor Rule and Suitability",
      "Advisers are fiduciaries, like custodians of UGMA/UTMA accounts, executors, and trustees. They must put the account owner first and invest prudently.\n\nThe Prudent Investor Rule says a fiduciary should:\n• Judge risk and return across the whole portfolio and the owner's broader goals\n• Diversify to avoid unneeded company-specific risk, unless that is not prudent\n• Avoid unreasonable fees and trading costs\n• Balance keeping money safe with keeping up with inflation; some risk is usually prudent\n• Hand off investment decisions to a broker-dealer, adviser, or bank trust department when appropriate\n\nSuitability means matching investments to the client: their finances, objectives, risk tolerance, and time horizon, and the investment's cost, risk, expected return, and growth or income features, all within the whole portfolio. If the client's information changes, the firm must update it and recommendations should change too.\n\nWithout a client's suitability information, a firm cannot make recommendations, but it can still take unsolicited orders. Blanket recommendations, telling everyone to buy something regardless of their situation, are banned.",
