      "s65-c9-conflicts",
      "Conflicts of Interest",
      "A conflict of interest is a reason to favor one interest over another, like the firm over a client, or one client over another. Material conflicts must be disclosed in writing before any recommendation.\n\nLoans. An adviser or IAR may borrow from a client only if the client is a broker-dealer, an affiliate, or a lender. It may lend to a client only if it is in the lending business or the client is an affiliate. Broker-dealer agents may not borrow from or lend to customers, though broker-dealers may lend in margin accounts.\n\nPrincipal trades. When an adviser that is also a broker-dealer sells to a client from its own inventory, it must disclose its role and the conflict, get the client's written permission before settlement, and send a confirmation showing its role and the client's consent.\n\nAgency cross trades. When the firm acts for both buyer and seller, it needs written consent from both after full disclosure, a confirmation at or before each trade, and a yearly written report of how many such trades it did and the commissions earned. It may charge one side an advisory fee and the other a commission, but not advisory fees to both.\n\nAdvisers must disclose when they recommend a security the firm owns, or when they trade against their own advice. Doing something banned through another person is still a violation.\n\nSelling away is getting paid for securities business your broker-dealer does not know about. An agent must tell the firm about outside activities; the firm can allow them with written permission and supervision.\n\nAn agent opening an account at another broker-dealer must tell that firm in writing, get written consent from their employer, and the other firm must send duplicate statements and confirmations to the employer on request.",
